✅ Privileged-field completeness guard: scanned 193 insert schemas, 27 body-reachable — no unclassified privileged column exposed across the 6 named families (role, status/approval, fee/payout/rate, verification, payment-identity, plan/entitlement), 15 grandfathered.
   NEGATIVE SPACE (ruling 43 — what this guard does NOT cover):
   · Scans shared/schema.ts createInsertSchema(...) call sites ONLY — a hand-written zod object
     schema, or a table in shared/guest-invites-schema.ts, is out of scope.
   · "Body-reachable" tracks req.body-derived identifiers ONE hop through a same-handler
     destructure or function call (the `extractServiceLocation(bodyWithoutX)` shape) — a value
     threaded through TWO or more intermediate calls, reassigned under yet another name, or
     passed into a helper declared in a different file is invisible to this pass. Each handler's
     taint set is scoped to that handler's own line range (delimited by the next app./router.
     registration) so it cannot leak across routes — but also cannot see across them.
   · Column-NAME family membership only. It does not know whether a route or storage layer
     already strips a flagged column server-side, and a grandfathered column is a human claim,
     not a machine proof.
   · The six families are NAMED, not exhaustive — an authorization grant, an arbitrary amount
     column, or any future privileged shape nobody has named yet is invisible until it becomes
     a seventh family here.
   · Does not analyze whether a route that parses a flagged schema ever actually spreads the
     field into a write — money-endpoints.cjs's line-level req.body pass owns that.
